WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.080
You can immediately start holding on to your&nbsp;
papers, because today we are going to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.160
a journey around the globe and simulate the&nbsp;
weather over the mountainous coast of Yucatan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.080
a beautiful day in the Swiss Alps, and&nbsp;
a searing day in the Sahara desert.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.680
Yes, from now on all of this is possible&nbsp;
through the power of this new paper. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.440
to showcase how quickly things are improving&nbsp;
in the wondrous land of computer graphics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.360
first, we were able to simulate a&nbsp;
tree. And then, researchers learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.360
to then simulate an entire forest. But&nbsp;
that’s not all, then, more recent papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.720
explore simulating an entire ecosystem for&nbsp;
thousands of years. Now that’s something.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:59.760
So, are we done here? What else is there to do?&nbsp;
Well, this new work finally helps us simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.960
weatherscapes, and it can go from&nbsp;
a light rain to a huge snowstorm.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.680
Now, previous methods were also&nbsp;
able to do something like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:17.040
but something was missing. For instance,&nbsp;
here, this looks great, but, the buoyancy part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:25.040
is just not there. Why? Well, let’s look at&nbsp;
the new method and find out together! Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:31.600
the new work simulates the microphysics of&nbsp;
water, which helps modeling phase changes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.680
buoyancy properly. You are seeing the buoyancy&nbsp;
part here. That makes a great difference.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.480
So, what about phase changes? Well,&nbsp;
look at this simulation. This is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:51.120
missing something. What is it missing? Well, the&nbsp;
simulation of the Foehn effect! What this means is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:58.000
a phenomenon where condensed warm air approaches&nbsp;
one side of the mountain, and something happens…or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.480
to be more exact, something doesn’t happen.&nbsp;
In reality, this should not get through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:12.240
So, let’s see the new simulation! Oh yes!&nbsp;
It is indeed stuck. Now that’s what I call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.360
a proper simulation of heat&nbsp;
transfer and phase changes. Bravo.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.920
And here you also see another example of a proper&nbsp;
phase change simulation see where the rainwater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.560
flows down the mountain to the ground, and the&nbsp;
new system simulates how it evaporates, wait for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.240
it…yes! It starts to form new clouds! So good.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.640
And it can simulate many more amazing&nbsp;
natural phenomena beyond these in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.920
that resembles reality quite well. My&nbsp;
favorites here are the mammatus clouds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.280
a phenomenon where the air descends and&nbsp;
warms, creating differences in temperatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.840
in the cloud, and this is what the&nbsp;
instability it creates looks like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:06.080
and, oh my! The hole punch! This can emerge due&nbsp;
to the air cooling rapidly, often due to a passing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:12.720
aircraft. All of these phenomena require the&nbsp;
simulations of the microphysics of water. Bravo!

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.200
So, microphysics huh? That&nbsp;
sounds computationally expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:25.120
so, let’s pop the question - how long do we have&nbsp;
to wait for a simulation like this? Are you seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:32.640
what I am seeing? How is that even possible? This&nbsp;
runs easily interactively, with approximately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:40.640
10 frames per second. It simulates this&nbsp;
and this and all of these variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:47.920
and it can do all this ten times every&nbsp;
second? That is insanity. Sign me up!

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:55.440
Now, one more thing. Views are not everything. Not&nbsp;
even close. However, I couldn’t ignore the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.800
that as of the making of this video&nbsp;
only approximately 500 people watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:07.040
the original paper video. I think that this is&nbsp;
such a beautiful work, everyone has to see it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.080
Before Two Minute Papers, I was just running&nbsp;
around at the university with these papers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.480
my hand trying to show them to the people I know.&nbsp;
And today, this is why Two Minute Papers exists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:25.360
and it can only exist with such an amazing and&nbsp;
receptive audience as you are. So thank you so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:31.120
much for coming with us on this glorious journey,&nbsp;
and make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.400
if you wish to see more outstanding works like&nbsp;
this, we have a great deal more coming up for you.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

